Node Affinity in Kubernetes is a scheduling feature that allows you to constrain which nodes your Pod can be scheduled on based on node labels. It is an advanced, more expressive version of the basic nodeSelector field, supporting complex matching rules, soft preferences, and conditional operators.


Core Types of Node Affinity

Kubernetes offers two main types of node affinity, defining whether a rule is a strict requirement or a soft preference.

Affinity Type	                                Behavior	                    Scheduling Impact
requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution: Hard requirement. The scheduler must meet the rule to place the Pod on a node. -> If no matching node exists, the Pod remains in a Pending state.


preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution: Soft preference. The scheduler tries to find a matching node, assigning higher priority to nodes that match.m -> If no matching node is available, the scheduler still places the Pod on another available node.

(Note: The IgnoredDuringExecution:  suffix means that if node labels change after the Pod is already running on that node, Kubernetes will not evict the Pod. It only evaluates the rules at scheduling time.)

