Logic Rules: AND vs. OR

Understanding how Kubernetes evaluates multiple rules is critical to avoiding unexpected scheduling failures:
• Multiple nodeSelectorTerms (OR logic): If you list multiple items under nodeSelectorTerms, the Pod can be scheduled onto a node if at least one of the terms is satisfied.
• Multiple matchExpressions (AND logic): Within a single nodeSelectorTerm, if you list multiple matchExpressions, all of them must be satisfied for the node to match.


1. AND Logic: Multiple matchExpressions

When you put multiple items under a single matchExpressions block, you are telling Kubernetes that a node must satisfy every single condition to qualify.
Goal: Schedule a Pod on a node that is in us-east-1a AND has an ssd disk.

nodeAffinity:
  requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
    nodeSelectorTerms:
    - matchExpressions:            # <--- ONE term starts here
      - key: zone                  # Condition 1
        operator: In
        values:
        - us-east-1a
      - key: disktype              # Condition 2 (ANDed with Condition 1)
        operator: In
        values:
        - ssd

• Node A (zone=us-east-1a, disktype=ssd) \(\rightarrow \) Matches. Both conditions are true.
• Node B (zone=us-east-1a, disktype=hdd) \(\rightarrow \) Fails. It fails the second condition.
• Node C (zone=us-west-1a, disktype=ssd) \(\rightarrow \) Fails. It fails the first condition.


2. OR Logic: Multiple nodeSelectorTerms

When you create multiple list items under nodeSelectorTerms, you are giving Kubernetes alternative paths. A node only needs to satisfy one of these blocks to qualify.
Goal: Schedule a Pod on a node that is in us-east-1a OR has an ssd disk (regardless of zone).

nodeAffinity:
  requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
    nodeSelectorTerms:
    - matchExpressions:            # <--- Term 1 starts here
      - key: zone
        operator: In
        values:
        - us-east-1a
    - matchExpressions:            # <--- Term 2 starts here (ORed with Term 1)
      - key: disktype
        operator: In
        values:
        - ssd

• Node A (zone=us-east-1a, disktype=ssd) \(\rightarrow \) Matches. Satisfies both Term 1 and Term 2.
• Node B (zone=us-east-1a, disktype=hdd) \(\rightarrow \) Matches. Satisfies Term 1.
• Node C (zone=us-west-1a, disktype=ssd) \(\rightarrow \) Matches. Satisfies Term 2.
• Node D (zone=us-west-1a, disktype=hdd) \(\rightarrow \) Fails. Satisfies neither term.


3. Combined Example: (A AND B) OR (C AND D)

In real-world clusters, you often combine both rules to handle fallback hardware or multi-region setups.
Goal: Run a heavy machine-learning Pod on either a high-CPU AWS node (cloud=aws AND cpu=high) OR a GPU-enabled GCP node (cloud=gcp AND gpu=true).

nodeAffinity:
  requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
    nodeSelectorTerms:
    # OPTION 1: AWS + High CPU
    - matchExpressions:
      - key: cloud
        operator: In
        values: ["aws"]
      - key: cpu
        operator: In
        values: ["high"]
    # OPTION 2: GCP + GPU
    - matchExpressions:
      - key: cloud
        operator: In
        values: ["gcp"]
      - key: gpu
        operator: In
        values: ["true"]

• Node 1 (cloud=aws, cpu=high) \(\rightarrow \) Matches Option 1.
• Node 2 (cloud=gcp, gpu=true) \(\rightarrow \) Matches Option 2.
• Node 3 (cloud=aws, gpu=true) \(\rightarrow \) Fails. It mixes conditions across different terms, satisfying neither complete option.


values List Also Uses OR Logic

Within a single matchExpression, listing multiple items under values also acts as an OR check for that specific key.

- key: zone
  operator: In
  values:
  - us-east-1a    # Matches us-east-1a OR us-east-1b
  - us-east-1b

