Weights in Preferred Affinity

When using "preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution", each rule has a weight field ranging from 1 to 100.
• During scheduling, the kube-scheduler evaluates all nodes that pass the hard requirements.
• For each preferred rule a node satisfies, its weight is added to the node's total score.
• The node with the highest combined score (factoring in other scheduling priorities) wins the Pod.

Here is a manifest combining both a hard requirement and a weighted soft preference:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: gpu-analytics-pod
spec:
  affinity:
    nodeAffinity:
      # HARD RULE: Must run in us-east-1a or us-east-1b AND have an SSD
      requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
        nodeSelectorTerms:
        - matchExpressions:
          - key: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
            operator: In
            values:
            - us-east-1a
            - us-east-1b
          - key: disktype
            operator: In
            values:
            - ssd
      # SOFT RULE: Strongly prefer nodes with a high-end GPU
      preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
      - weight: 80
        preference:
          matchExpressions:
          - key: gpu-type
            operator: In
            values:
            - nvidia-a100
  containers:
  - name: worker
    image: nginx:latest

When you deploy this Pod, the kube-scheduler runs a two-step pipeline—first filtering out unsuitable nodes, and then scoring the remaining ones—to decide where gpu-analytics-pod will run.

Step 1: Filtering (The Hard Rule)

First, the scheduler evaluates requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution. It checks every node in your cluster against this single nodeSelectorTerm.

Because both expressions sit inside the same matchExpressions list, a node must pass both checks:
1. Zone Check: Does the node have the label topology.kubernetes.io/zone set to us-east-1a OR us-east-1b?
2. Disk Check: Does the node ALSO have the label disktype set to ssd? 

• If NO nodes pass both checks: The scheduler stops here. Your Pod stays stuck in the Pending state until a matching node becomes available.
• If 1 or more nodes pass: Only those qualifying nodes move forward to Step 2. All other nodes are eliminated.

Step 2: Scoring (The Soft Rule)

Next, the scheduler takes the surviving nodes from Step 1 and evaluates preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution.
• It checks each surviving node for the label gpu-type=nvidia-a100.
• If a node has it: The scheduler adds +80 points to that node's overall score.
• If a node does not have it: The node gets 0 bonus points, but it is not disqualified.
The scheduler then combines these affinity points with its default built-in scoring rules (such as available CPU/RAM and spreading Pods evenly across nodes) and places the Pod on the node with the highest total score.

Real-World Cluster Walkthrough

Imagine your cluster has these 4 nodes when you apply this YAML:

Node	            Labels	            Passes Step 1 (Filter)? 	Step 2 Score Bonus	    Final Outcome
Node-1 ->zone=us-east-1a, disktype=ssd, gpu-type=nvidia-a100 ->Yes (Zone + SSD) ->	+80 -.	Winner. Meets hard rules and gets the GPU bonus.

Node-2 -> zone=us-east-1b, disktype=ssd, gpu-type=nvidia-t4	-> Yes (Zone + SSD) ->	+0	-> Backup. Used if Node-1 is full or down, even without the A100 GPU.

Node-3	-> zone=us-east-1a, disktype=hdd, gpu-type=nvidia-a100	-> No (Fails disktype=ssd)	-> Eliminated	Rejected. -> Even though it has the A100 GPU, failing the hard rule disqualifies it immediately.

Node-4	-> zone=us-west-2a, disktype=ssd, gpu-type=nvidia-a100	-> No (Fails zone)	-> Eliminated	Rejected. Wrong availability zone.


Step 3: After Scheduling (IgnoredDuringExecution)

Once the Pod starts running on Node-1, Kubernetes stops enforcing these affinity rules for that Pod.
• If an administrator removes the disktype=ssd label from Node-1 ten minutes later, Kubernetes will not evict or kill gpu-analytics-pod.
• The rules only apply again if the Pod is deleted and recreated.


